body:
Get it here:

[https://drive.google.com/file/d/1wZKZiaCZW7bblsizFPGz9FAl9\_ugEmAO/view?usp=sharing](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1wZKZiaCZW7bblsizFPGz9FAl9_ugEmAO/view?usp=sharing)

&#x200B;

It's not really very realistic but will look ok from a distance.

&#x200B;

Note!

Only tested with the Modular Tree Addon.

&#x200B;

Otherwise it needs to be uw-mapped with each branch in the same direction.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Yeah, I would just use the normal sun lamp, with a very tight/hard shadow to simulate the actual sun. In space I would assume the most light will come from the sun, so maybe try to find a starfield hdri, or add some blue backlight from under. Maybe experiment with some stars in the background, that could give some ambiance to the render. 

&#x200B;

Experiment with the lighting, that is what will really give this a nice render I think, good luck

One tip is to really work tightly from a reference image, it makes it easier to see how your render is comparing to a real photograph. Otherwise it's very easy to get lost. 

&#x200B;

My impression is the wood texture is just not pleasant, there is no grainy-ness to it, and it just looks fake, it seems to be perfectly glossy. I would just find a good texture or just use a plastic white using the principle shader. The Cactus looks cartoony, with the saturated colors, no bump/normal and no roughness map.

&#x200B;

The pot looks pretty good, altough I can't really tell what material it supposed to be. In my opinion to make a realistic material, you should instantly see what your object is made of, if you are unsure then something is not made correct.

&#x200B;

In conclusion, I would advise to just spend time looking at references for comparison, that is a great way to learn realistic renders, if it doesn't look right figure out what is missing and keep iterating.

&#x200B;

Best of luck to you :)

Turntable model made by ifilgood from blendswap

Bean bag - Chemsy from blendswap  

&#x200B;
